{"qid":"Q2195101","names":["Principality of Guria"],"title":"Principality of Guria","endonym":{"native":"გურიის სამთავრო","lang":"ka"},"capsule":"The Principality of Guria (გურიის სამთავრო) was a Georgian feudal state on the eastern Black Sea coast, carved out of the disintegrating Kingdom of Georgia around 1468 under the Gurieli dynastic house. At its height in the sixteenth century it controlled the coastal lowlands between the Rioni and Chorokhi rivers and resisted Ottoman encroachment through a combination of vassalage and diplomacy. Repeated Ottoman raids from the late sixteenth century progressively reduced its autonomy, and the principality nominally submitted to the Russian Empire in 1810–1811. Russia formally abolished the Gurieli line in 1829–1830, annexing the territory directly into the Kutaisi guberniya.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Territorial extent is broadly attested for the early modern period through Georgian chronicles and Russian annexation documents, but precise boundaries before the seventeenth century are poorly mapped.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Principality of Guria","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Principality_of_Guria"},{"label":"Britannica: Guria","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Guria"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Principality of Guria","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Kakhaber II Gurieli","nativeName":"კახაბერ გურიელი","roman":"Kakhaber Gurieli","start":1469,"end":1483,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Giorgi I Gurieli","nativeName":"გიორგი გურიელი","roman":"Giorgi Gurieli","start":1483